Intense pain immediately after knee replacement surgery is a natural part of the process. Your medical team will actively manage your pain with painkillers and nerve blocks, so don't try to endure it; follow their instructions. The first few days are focused on acute pain control and joint stabilization. From 2 to 4 weeks after surgery, pain will noticeably decrease, and rehabilitation will begin in earnest. Temporary pain during exercise is an essential part of joint recovery and muscle strengthening. Only consistent exercise and active rehabilitation will enable a quick recovery. Most people experience significant relief from pain between 3 and 6 months. Using your knee in daily life will become comfortable, and you will be able to resume previously restricted activities. Even if you occasionally feel a throbbing sensation, it will be manageable, and your quality of life will significantly improve. Complete recovery and maximum pain reduction can take 6 months to over a year. The artificial joint will fully adapt to your body, surrounding muscles will strengthen, and stability will increase. Consistent management and healthy lifestyle habits are key to regaining an active daily life.
